I'm going to go ahead and exit my repel and then run this file in the terminal.

01:26.360 --> 01:36.320
So I have a list here, 4, 3, 4, 5 that relates to the lengths of each of these strings

01:36.320 --> 01:37.840
in the list.

01:37.840 --> 01:43.480
A list comprehension is going to let us do the same thing in one line pretty simply

01:43.480 --> 01:44.880
and easily.

01:44.880 --> 01:52.480
So here I'm going to print before followed by my list.

01:52.480 --> 01:56.880
And then I'm going to write my list comprehension.

01:56.880 --> 02:05.680
So a list comprehension, I find it easiest to write kind of in reverse order.

02:05.680 --> 02:12.040
So I'm going to start with the square brackets because I know that I want a list comprehension

02:12.040 --> 02:15.040
and square brackets are the syntax for list.

02:15.040 --> 02:18.800
And then I'm going to type in what I'm looping over.

02:18.800 --> 02:24.080
So I'm going to type in 4 name in names.

02:24.080 --> 02:32.000
Once I do that, I'm going to move my cursor all the way to the left to just after the

02:32.000 --> 02:35.120
opening square bracket.

02:35.120 --> 02:42.400
This is where I type in what result I want from looping over this set.

02:42.400 --> 02:45.160
Sorry, looping over these values.

02:45.160 --> 02:49.640
In this case, I want the length of names.

02:49.640 --> 02:51.360
And that is a list comprehension.

02:51.360 --> 02:53.600
It replaces all of this.

02:53.600 --> 03:05.320
And now I can print out after and run this and get the same thing.

03:05.320 --> 03:17.320
So I can assign it to a variable as well and get the same result.

03:17.320 --> 03:22.640
I think this is pretty clean and readable and understandable.

03:22.640 --> 03:28.280
It is one of the things that folks for new to Python tend to trip over quite a bit.

03:28.280 --> 03:34.360
And the best way to get over that hump is to just practice as much as possible.

03:34.360 --> 03:41.120
So typing in these exercises and getting that muscle memory in, I find that trick of writing

03:41.120 --> 03:47.200
my list comprehension in reverse really helps me remember, okay, I need the square brackets

03:47.200 --> 03:48.200
because it's a list.

03:48.200 --> 03:51.120
I need to figure out what I'm looping over.

03:51.120 --> 03:56.360
And then I need to figure out what do I want to extract.

03:56.360 --> 04:00.760
In this case, it's the length of the names.

04:00.800 --> 04:08.520
We can also have conditional statements in these list comprehension.

04:08.520 --> 04:13.800
If we want to add a condition, we move our cursor all the way to the right hand side.

04:13.800 --> 04:15.720
And that's where our condition goes.

04:15.720 --> 04:18.360
The condition is optional.

04:18.360 --> 04:26.560
And here you can type if and an expression that either evaluates to true or false.

04:26.560 --> 04:33.120
When the expression is true, that value on the left hand side will end up in the list comprehension.

04:33.120 --> 04:36.480
If that expression is false, it will not.

04:36.480 --> 04:47.160
So if I want to only get the, let's say the names that have even numbers of characters,

04:47.160 --> 04:55.000
I can say, remove this and say, what I actually want is the name itself.

04:55.000 --> 05:02.000
And then have a condition that says, if the length of the name, modular to means,

05:02.000 --> 05:08.800
is the length of the name even equal to zero, then put it in my list.

05:08.800 --> 05:17.320
So now when I run this, I will only see the names that have an even number of characters.

05:17.320 --> 05:24.960
If I flip this condition and say, not equals and run this, then I will get the other

05:24.960 --> 05:31.200
two names, the one that had the odd numbers of characters.

05:31.200 --> 05:35.920
There are ways of moving between different types in Python.

05:35.920 --> 05:37.520
One is split.

05:37.520 --> 05:45.920
So if you have a string that is separated by commas and you want to extract those values,

05:45.920 --> 05:47.920
you can use split.

05:47.920 --> 05:50.480
So going back to the repal.

05:50.480 --> 06:02.240
If I have a name string that looks something like this, you might see something like this

06:02.240 --> 06:05.760
in a CSV file if you ever work with those.

06:05.760 --> 06:13.720
If you want to get a list of names out of this string, you can call the dot split method

06:13.720 --> 06:22.200
on it and pass in the token that you want to split on.

06:22.200 --> 06:28.160
If you have spaces in between those names, then you would need to pass in a token that

06:28.160 --> 06:30.960
is a comma in a space as well.

06:30.960 --> 06:38.680
So this is one very quick and easy way of moving between a string and a list.

06:38.680 --> 06:44.360
If you then wanted to save these values in a CSV file.

06:44.360 --> 06:53.600
So, in this case, I have not modified my name string, remember strings are immutable.

06:53.600 --> 07:02.320
So let's say I called this a names list and I said name strings dot split on comma.

07:02.320 --> 07:04.120
Now I have a list.

07:04.200 --> 07:13.480
If I wanted to get that back into a string, I can call the dot join method on a string.

07:13.480 --> 07:16.880
In this case, it's going to look a little funky.

07:16.880 --> 07:22.000
I'm going to use space and then a dash and a space.

07:22.000 --> 07:28.360
And I'm going to say dot join with my names list.

07:28.360 --> 07:35.960
And that's going to return a string that has this millimeter.

07:35.960 --> 07:42.680
You can also use this string joining with a list comprehension as well.

07:42.680 --> 07:59.280
So let's say that I wanted to multiply each number in a list by two.

07:59.280 --> 08:07.680
I could use a list comprehension for that and say four names in my names and move my cursor

08:07.680 --> 08:14.080
all the way to the left side and say num times two.

08:14.080 --> 08:17.680
That's going to be a list comprehension.

08:17.680 --> 08:31.000
I can call that join with this list comprehension sorry that did not copyright.

08:31.040 --> 08:36.040
I can call that join with this list comprehension here.

08:36.040 --> 08:39.800
And it will accomplish the same thing.

08:39.800 --> 08:40.800
Oops, sorry.

08:40.800 --> 08:46.280
I need to convert this into a string.

08:46.280 --> 08:48.280
There.

08:48.280 --> 08:54.440
So moving between types and Python is really quite simple.

08:54.440 --> 09:00.600
And if any of you have done any coding challenges like the advent of code calendar,

09:00.640 --> 09:06.240
you'll find that a lot of programmers who do that will reach for Python because moving

09:06.240 --> 09:09.960
between types is so simple and straightforward.

09:09.960 --> 09:15.160
And that's not necessarily the case in other programming languages.

09:15.160 --> 09:22.360
There are other kinds of comprehension like dictionary comprehension set comprehension.

09:22.360 --> 09:26.240
I'm not going to talk about those during this course.

09:26.320 --> 09:31.920
There is a chapter available in the course website that you can read on your own and there is

09:31.920 --> 09:35.400
practice available as well.

09:35.400 --> 09:41.520
What I do want to chat about though is generators.

09:41.520 --> 09:48.120
Generator expressions are, well, we've already seen some of those generator expressions.

09:48.120 --> 09:53.640
Like when we tried to call range, we didn't just get back a full list of all the numbers

09:53.680 --> 09:55.160
that range generated.

09:55.160 --> 09:58.920
Instead, we got back a different kind of function.

09:58.920 --> 10:02.440
So a generator is a more advanced concept.

10:02.440 --> 10:08.040
It's like an object that you're able to iterate over like a list.

10:08.040 --> 10:15.800
But unlike a list, a generator only knows about where it is now and how to get the next value.

10:15.800 --> 10:21.000
And it does that on demand without generating that list of values all at once.

10:21.000 --> 10:25.560
So a generator comprehension does look a lot like a list comprehension,

10:25.560 --> 10:30.760
except it uses a single pair of parentheses instead of brackets.

10:30.760 --> 10:43.400
So here's a list comprehension where we are generating squares for every number,

10:43.400 --> 10:47.320
under 10, if that number is even.

10:47.400 --> 10:57.800
I could change this to a generator comprehension by changing these square brackets to parentheses.

10:57.800 --> 11:04.680
If I check the type of my list comprehension, sorry, I'm going to call this my generator comprehension.

11:04.680 --> 11:10.040
If I check the type of my generator comprehension, we'll see that it is a generator.

11:10.040 --> 11:14.520
And if I try to look at it, it's going to come back with that.

11:15.480 --> 11:19.320
If I wanted to see what was in this generator, I could call list on it.

11:20.520 --> 11:26.600
But generally, when you are using a generator, you don't have a need to have all of those numbers or

11:26.600 --> 11:31.800
values or whatever and memory all at once. Instead, you probably want to loop through them and do

11:31.800 --> 11:40.840
something with them. So I can loop over every item in this generator comprehension and print it out.

11:41.720 --> 11:47.080
And I can do that just like I would with a list, that syntax is seamless.

11:50.840 --> 11:56.760
The thing about generators, though, is that once you use a generator, again, it doesn't have any

11:56.760 --> 12:03.480
state, it's not stored in memory. So once you loop over all these items, the generator is exhausted.

12:04.600 --> 12:08.840
It doesn't know how to get back to whatever state it was in beforehand.

12:09.480 --> 12:15.800
So if I try to loop over this again, I'm not going to get anything back.

12:16.840 --> 12:23.640
I've used up my generator and if I want to use it again, I have to recreate it and start from scratch.

12:25.800 --> 12:33.640
So as Python has migrated to Python 3, a lot more of these internal structures have moved

12:33.640 --> 12:39.720
over to be generators or generator like. And even though this is a more advanced concept,

12:39.720 --> 12:45.800
it's really important to know about because of those optimizations that have happened over the years.

